
  Using [38;5;153mFormData[39m is why inputs need [38;5;153mname[39m attributes: it means [38;5;153mbindForm[39m never hardcodes field names or calls
  [38;5;153mgetElementById[39m, which is exactly what made the current code single-purpose. Form #2 becomes a rules object and a
  callback.

  [1m4. Error handling[0m

  [38;5;153mvalidate[39m never throws; invalid input is data, not an exception. A field named in [38;5;153mrules[39m with no matching input yields
  [38;5;153mundefined[39m, which [38;5;153mrequired[39m correctly fails. [38;5;153mbindForm[39m throws immediately with a clear message if [38;5;153mformEl[39m is null — an
  improvement on today's behavior, where a missing [38;5;153m#login-form[39m crashes with an opaque [38;5;153maddEventListener of null[39m.
  [38;5;153monInvalid[39m defaults to logging to [38;5;153mconsole.error[39m, preserving the current app's behavior exactly.

  [1m5. Testing, and one honest gap[0m

  [38;5;153mvalidation.test.mjs[39m covers each validator in isolation, [38;5;153mvalidate[39m's aggregation, first-error-per-field precedence, and
  cross-field access via [38;5;153mallValues[39m. All pure functions, no DOM needed.

  [1mThe gap:[0m [38;5;153mbindForm[39m touches the DOM, and testing it needs jsdom — a dependency you ruled out. So the design pushes all
  logic into [38;5;153mvalidate[39m and keeps [38;5;153mbindForm[39m to ~6 lines of glue, which I'll verify manually in a browser (serve over HTTP,
  submit empty → errors logged; submit filled → login result logged). That glue will have no automated coverage. If that
  bothers you, the fix is adding jsdom as a devDependency — say so and I'll fold it in.

  [1mOut of scope:[0m rendering errors into the page, async validators, lint/format, e2e, and any change to [38;5;153msrc/[39m.
